#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069834} :activity/id #uuid "37643965-adaf-5a8f-893a-965115fd6336" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372 :topic/id #uuid "43f7df3c-d8d4-556e-9f46-8a4092e26bff" :topic/math-academy-id 450} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069834 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069995 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + {\\color{blue}{a}}^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNot all quadratic expressions are perfect squares. However, every quadratic expression can be expressed as a perfect square *plus a constant*. In this lesson, we'll learn how to write quadratic expressions in this form.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 6x + 3^2\\right) + 4\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! We have successfully written the original expression as a perfect square $(x + {\\color{blue}3})^2$ plus a constant $(4).$\n\nWriting a quadratic expression as a perfect square plus a constant is called **completing the square.** Completing the square is a valuable technique for solving many problems.  \n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "b0bfd351-3f52-57d4-9600-4acfe8b40ed1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6211 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "273bce78-1551-5eaf-9359-3a2c47ce2622" :step/math-academy-id 458036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069835}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069835 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068672 :question/id #uuid "2acac780-2b17-5489-accc-2a3a090784e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9927"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049026}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511881} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069051} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290587} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290588} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290589} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532699}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"} :step/id #uuid "31f14f81-4bd6-571c-8b99-aa9301e20ac2" :step/math-academy-id 458037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068677 :question/id #uuid "b107ed10-4501-50ad-8ad7-231fd933bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-9997"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511910} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511892} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511908} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549954}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"} :step/id #uuid "9ecc4781-e6b1-5e4e-8652-11ca304fe429" :step/math-academy-id 458041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069837}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069837 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069946 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've completed the square on quadratic expressions whose perfect square is already isolated. \n\nIn general, isolating the perfect square in a quadratic expression requires a trick. We'll now learn how this works.\n\nAs an example, let's complete the square on the following expression:\n\n$$\nx^2+10x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term ($10$) as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x+{\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nSo, the linear coefficient is now decomposed into two factors, $\\color{red}2$ and ${\\color{blue}{5}}.$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and then *subtract* the square of the second factor ${\\color{blue}{5}},$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{15}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{5}}x + {\\color{blue}{5}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n(x+{\\color{blue}{5}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{5}}^2 + {\\color{purple}{15}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the last two terms as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-25+15 & = \\\\ (x+5)^{2}-10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we're done!\n\nWe can easily check that our answer is correct by expanding the parentheses and simplifying:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+25-10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+15 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a88e59c6-54ce-5658-8ef7-3ae4b4ac3f09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1627 :tutorial/title "Completing the Square"} :step/id #uuid "4e6c408c-3d82-5cd5-8ebb-a93b3090c029" :step/math-academy-id 458044 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069838}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069838 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068429 :question/id #uuid "aa51996a-09e5-5950-8438-795ab3e0f027" :question/math-academy-id "e-10008"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511822} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272299} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292540} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511812} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532698}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"} :step/id #uuid "99bc26c8-9042-5e18-8fb2-209deab8257d" :step/math-academy-id 458045 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069839}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069839 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068430 :question/id #uuid "06ed73b3-1442-5b21-a317-961d0395f7da" :question/math-academy-id "e-10009"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511771} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290597} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532681}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"} :step/id #uuid "e2c01f2d-d447-54c1-a47b-3f57a1a74c64" :step/math-academy-id 458048}] :activity/title "Completing the Square" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068977}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550051} :activity/id #uuid "b0fb9cbe-a644-586a-b7c5-2e3cbc97298d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050372 :topic/id #uuid "43f7df3c-d8d4-556e-9f46-8a4092e26bff" :topic/math-academy-id 450} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549954 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b94ed9c-f4fb-5037-a3fd-676575385f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549335} {:answer/id #uuid "239e272e-e941-51b1-88d1-658092cfdf99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y-24)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549389} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6d081e-5e34-5799-885e-d02bf5b30f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y-12)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549440} {:answer/id #uuid "4b420642-8b38-528a-b9fd-458b11392903" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y+12)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549468} {:answer/id #uuid "90614b3a-fb15-561e-801a-0578deafc573" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(y+24)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549598}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549440} :answer-field/id #uuid "1776e3b4-56ca-52fb-a385-2675941436c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3846b0e4-266a-5fcc-97c9-5ac90ce8b7bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-140285" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}-24y+144)+5$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2ay+{a}^{2}=(y-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-24y+144 & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-24y+{12}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}-2\\cdot 12y+{12}^{2} & = \\\\ (y-12)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}-24y+144)+5 & = \\\\ (y-12)^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "46213224-80fa-53d0-9106-cff102084b4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272045 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "676795d7-785f-5397-8327-127950122542" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-11)^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549513} {:answer/id #uuid "6d0cbb7e-2e2b-5c3e-9916-24ba60cdd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+11)^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549524} {:answer/id #uuid "782c0f56-ce37-5099-a0fe-148c3a2874bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+12)^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549545} {:answer/id #uuid "92ce75a4-5626-5103-ac1f-efecdd50c67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-22)^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549604} {:answer/id #uuid "e423200f-b6b6-53b6-8137-df750e0ae04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x-12)^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549736}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549513} :answer-field/id #uuid "90eb344c-2847-58a2-8769-e020f945532e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff51e775-e2b4-585e-a683-f90782853abe" :question/math-academy-id "q-140283" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-11)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\ (x-11)^{2}-4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "46931b01-d215-5f84-b5da-4675f952102f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53ee2aa7-49df-5b3a-8072-6c045e66c322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+10)^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549484} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5616f0-230f-5380-b162-0895d7287876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549580} {:answer/id #uuid "a61c1956-9aa0-5bda-b2b7-c15d6dd5eb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549628} {:answer/id #uuid "adae3f5a-4f4b-5c8d-b4ef-48df06cebd7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549640} {:answer/id #uuid "b69ae637-47df-5d0a-a406-5a5d5619d853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549655} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b561ff-d7d5-56bf-bd70-881cdbd1aea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c7d3420b-3f28-5142-8868-756f1394ea88" :question/math-academy-id "q-141537" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+10x+16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+16\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+5)^{2}-9.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b79bb64-c64c-594d-b208-8d0b50578499"}] :activity/title "Completing the Square" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549311}]}
